---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Quickstart"
  headings={[]}
  source="site/src/pages/docs/quickstart.astro"
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Edit model weights with Python</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Access named tensors, evaluate your changes, and save a standalone
      checkpoint.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use Python 3.12 or later in a virtual environment. You need a local
      Hugging Face causal-language-model snapshot with its tokenizer files. WSM
      does not download model files or execute model repository Python.
    </p>
    <Code code={`python3 -m pip install 'wsm[editing]==0.1.0'`} lang="bash" />
    <p>
      Replace the paths below with your snapshot and an unused checkpoint
      destination whose parent directory exists. The example requires a tensor
      named <code>model.norm.weight</code>. Inspect <code>model.weights</code>
      for your model's names and shapes before choosing an edit.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`from wsm import WeightModel

model = WeightModel.load("/path/to/snapshot")
cases = [{"prompt": "Say hello.", "target": "Hello!"}]
before = model.evaluate(cases)
model.weights["model.norm.weight"][:16].mul_(0.95)
after = model.evaluate(cases)
print({"baseline": before, "candidate": after})
model.save("/path/to/checkpoint")`}
      lang="python"
    />
    <p>
      Evaluation reports target-token negative log likelihood, not generated
      responses. This edit demonstrates the API without claiming to improve the
      model.
    </p>
    <p>
      To run the edit with the CLI, save the following as <code>edit.py</code>.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`def edit(model):
    model.weights["model.norm.weight"][:16].mul_(0.95)`}
      lang="python"
    />
    <p>
      Save a JSON array of prompt and target objects as <code>cases.json</code>.
    </p>
    <Code code={`[{"prompt": "Say hello.", "target": "Hello!"}]`} lang="json" />
    <p>
      Replace the snapshot path and choose an output directory that does not
      exist. Create its parent directory before running the command.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`mkdir -p runs
wsm execute edit.py --model /path/to/snapshot --cases cases.json --output runs/edit`}
      lang="bash"
    />
    <p>
      The runner records both evaluations in <code>runs/edit/run.json</code>
      and saves the checkpoint in <code>runs/edit/checkpoint/</code>. You decide
      whether to keep the change. Python editing files run with your process
      permissions and are not sandboxed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read the <a href="/docs/coding/">Python editing guide</a> for API details
      and execution permissions.
    </p>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
